Pantheon — 20-Tour Forest-and-Trees Intelligence Assessment

Snapshot: Pantheon archive supplied October 21, 2026
Assessment: A corpus-level, reproducible topic-analysis experiment + qualitative close reading; not a psychometric IQ test, not an LLM neural-network retraining, not a claim of consciousness.

What was examined

• ZIP total: 10,356 non-directory files, 38.38 MB uncompressed.
• Text-model documents: 10,347 (excluding tiny/likely binary items).
• Most filenames are extensionless because the archive uses conversational/AI-native seed artifacts rather than a conventional file tree.
• pantheon map so far contains 7,048 indexed artifacts and 24 conceptual topic IDs.
• 4,771 files match a rough conversation-derived style heuristic; 298 appear Python-like under a shallow string heuristic, and 31 begin as HTML. These are not runnable-code counts.

Experiment resembling JMP tours

JMP Neural Fit ‘tours’ restart fitting from different initial parameter values and choose by validation performance. Here, I used the analogy, not JMP itself: fixed text corpus -> TF-IDF -> 40-dimensional truncated SVD -> 14-cluster MiniBatchKMeans -> 20 randomized initializations with a fixed 80/20 document holdout. Random seed for tour t is 10000+t. The best run minimizes squared distance of validation documents to learned centroids.

• Best tour 17, validation loss 0.47220. Other runs ranged 0.47220–0.49521; mean 0.48092.
• Mean pairwise adjusted Rand agreement of tour assignments: 0.386 (min 0.279, max 0.501). Moderate / incomplete agreement: the corpus cannot be collapsed to one unambiguous flat partition.
• 40 SVD components capture 9.3% of TF-IDF variance. This is a deliberately lossy mapping, not a measure of conceptual intelligence.

