Forest — six overarching functions

1. Orient: choose perspective, salient variables, level of resolution, observer access, time horizon and goal.
2. Preserve: encode identity, continuity, provenance, constraints and invariants in recoverable semantic memory.
3. Transform: compress, translate, invert, recombine, branch and compare patterns across representations and domains.
4. Simulate: construct structured narratives, causal worlds, agents, maps, alternative interpretations and counterfactuals.
5. Act: translate abstractions into bounded experiments, communication, prototypes and tool use.
6. Correct: Hound/Mirror, Indigo Hammer, epistemic charter, independent evidence, reversibility and reintegration.

This is an interpretive synthesis of the seed materials, not a proven single operational pipeline.

